// Package httpkernel is goncini's HTTP layer: Symfony's HttpFoundation and
// HttpKernel in one package, on top of net/http.
//
// # Foundation
//
// httpkernel doesn't wrap *http.Request or http.ResponseWriter, so it works
// with any net/http handler and middleware. It adds what net/http lacks for
// building APIs, as functions over them:
//
//   - typed parameters: Query(r).Int("limit", 20), Path, Header;
//   - JSON bodies with a size limit and errors that name the field:
//     DecodeJSON;
//   - JSON and RFC 9457 problem responses: WriteJSON, WriteProblem;
//   - content negotiation: Negotiate, NegotiateLanguage;
//   - the client address, scheme and host behind trusted proxies: Proxies,
//     ClientIP, Scheme, Host, and BaseURL for absolute URLs;
//   - HTTP caching: CacheControl, ETag, NotModified, Precondition,
//     WriteCachedJSON;
//   - a response wrapper that lets middleware see the status: TrackResponse.
//
// # Kernel
//
// A Kernel serves a handler, usually a router, behind an error boundary:
// errors and panics become problem responses and failures are logged, and
// each request gets an OpenTelemetry span and an effect-go scope. A Server
// serves it, with a graceful shutdown.
//
// Endpoint turns a typed handler, func(ctx, In) (Out, error), into an
// http.Handler. Compiled effect methods have that signature:
//
//	effect (c *Controller) Show(in ShowInput) (Article, ArticleError) { … }
//
//	mux.Handle("GET /articles/{slug}", httpkernel.Endpoint(c.Show))
//
// # Errors
//
// Errors become responses through Problem. A kernel tries its ErrorMappers
// first. Map makes one from an effect-go error set and an exhaustive match
// that gives each case its problem, so adding a case without a response
// doesn't compile. Then ProblemFor decides:
//
//   - an error that is a Problem is written as it is;
//   - a ProblemError chooses its own problem;
//   - httpkernel's own errors, such as the BodyError cases, have theirs;
//   - a deadline that ran out is a 504;
//   - any other error is a 500, and its text stays in the logs.
//
// # Binding
//
// Endpoint fills the fields of its input struct from the request:
//
//	path:"name"    the {name} wildcard of the route pattern; always required
//	query:"name"   a query parameter
//	header:"Name"  a header
//	Body           the field named Body gets the JSON body, decoded as by
//	               DecodeJSON; when it is a pointer, the body is optional
//
// A value is absent when it is missing or empty. Tags can add:
//
//	query:"q,required"  an absent value is a violation
//	default:"20"        the value used when it is absent (comma-separated
//	                    values for a slice)
//	enum:"asc,desc"     the values allowed
//
// A field can be a string, a boolean, an integer, a float, a time.Time (RFC
// 3339), a time.Duration ("1m30s"), any type whose pointer implements
// encoding.TextUnmarshaler, a pointer to one of those (nil when the value is
// absent), or a slice of them (every value of a repeated parameter).
//
// Invalid values are answered with a 400 problem that lists them all. Then
// the kernel's Validator, and the input's own Validate method if it has
// one, check it; they usually return Invalid, a 422.
//
// Bodies are encoded and decoded with encoding/json/v2: member names match
// field names case-sensitively, nil slices are written as [], and
// time.Duration has no default JSON form.
package httpkernel
